tl;dr on today's drama is that cryptography generally assumes that people are pretty smart.

as it turns out we're actually pretty stupid.

but we've now made something that actually is pretty smart, so a lot of our assumptions about the universe may break, and in short order.

Now, if one of those assumptions turns out to be public key cryptography, then our precious coins are in trouble. It would be theoretically possible to derive a private key from a public key (which is exposed every time you transact).

@Modest_Dreams Correct. You'd have to do offchain links, like the ones that Glyph allows. 

But I would consider this whole thing a doomsday scenario in which you probably just don't want to be holding crypto if cryptography is broken.

Of course, this wouldn't just apply to crypto. It would be a full blown cybersecurity emergency. 

Websites, banking apps, encrypted traffic, passkeys, certificate-based authentication, private messaging, banks themselves, and more would be in danger of breaking.

